    "input": "{\"current_utc\": \"2026-09-24T18:37:18.243927+00:00\", \"assigned_topic\": {\"study_topic_id\": \"study-f5d7bba32cc065adb6f8\", \"subject_id\": \"subject-15848dffeb51d258a8e6\", \"topic\": \"Hawaiian Islands\", \"primary_category\": \"Geography\", \"description\": \"The Polynesian volcanic archipelago in the central Pacific that includes the U.S. state of Hawaii.\", \"subcategory_ids\": [\"geo-023\", \"geo-014\"], \"level_occurrence_counts\": {\"high-school\": 1, \"middle-school\": 1}, \"role_occurrence_counts\": {\"answer\": 2}, \"source_question_count\": 2, \"aliases\": [\"Hawaiian Archipelago\", \"Sandwich Islands\"], \"topic_type\": \"place\", \"source_ids\": [\"qb-387c038ad06630348ca93196\", \"qb-9219a5aa6318a64ef45dba8d\"], \"source_questions\": [{\"custom_id\": \"qb-387c038ad06630348ca93196\", \"level\": \"high-school\", \"question_type\": \"bonuses\", \"source\": \"2022 SHOW-ME\", \"round\": \"4\", \"number\": \"5\", \"parts\": [{\"label\": \"L\", \"question\": \"Some stories describe the origins of this activity as the volcano goddess Pele performing it to celebrate finding land in spite of Namakaokahaʻi [“nah-mah-koh-KAH-ah-ee”]. For 10 points each:\", \"answer\": \"\"}, {\"label\": \"A\", \"question\": \"[M] Name this activity whose ku’i genre involves oli chants. Performers of this activity wear kī leaf dresses and lei.\", \"answer\": \"hula dancing [prompt on dancing with “What type of dancing?”]\"}, {\"label\": \"B\", \"question\": \"[H] Pele sends Hi’iaka [“hee-ah-ka”] on a journey to Kawai’i to find a husband after being awoken by these jula instruments. Griots of West Africa sing mythical stories while playing one of these instruments called the djembe.\", \"answer\": \"drums\"}, {\"label\": \"C\", \"question\": \"[E] Hula dancing is a tradition in this archipelago, which was raised from the sea by Māui’s fish hook.\", \"answer\": \"Hawai’i [or the Hawaiian Archipelago]\"}]}, {\"custom_id\": \"qb-9219a5aa6318a64ef45dba8d\", \"level\": \"middle-school\", \"question_type\": \"bonuses\", \"source\": \"2022 SCOP MS 11\", \"round\": \"8\", \"number\": \"13\", \"parts\": [{\"label\": \"L\", \"question\": \"In these islands’ religion, the deity Ku’ula controls the fish in the sea. For ten points each,\", \"answer\": \"\"}, {\"label\": \"A\", \"question\": \"Name this set of islands, an American state whose native religion considers sites like Mauna Loa and Mauna Kea to be sacred.\", \"answer\": \"Hawai’i (accept Hawaiian Islands; prompt on “Polynesia”)\"}, {\"label\": \"B\", \"question\": \"This traditional dance in Hawaii is often accompanied by chant or song and was used for storytelling and rituals. Before performing, dancers may make offerings to Laka, a patron of this dance.\", \"answer\": \"hula\"}, {\"label\": \"C\", \"question\": \"This Goddess of volcanoes brought fire to the Hawaiian islands and lives in Mount Kilauea. During a sled race, she started a fight with the snow Goddess Poli’ahu [poh-lee-ah-hoo] due to jealousy.\", \"answer\": \"Pele [peh-leh] (accept Madame Pele or Tutu Pele)\"}]}]}}",
