            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-f5d7bba32cc065adb6f8\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"The Hawaiian Islands are a volcanic archipelago in the central Pacific and include the islands of the U.S. state of Hawaiʻi. Their land is the exposed summits of volcanic mountains built up from the ocean floor by repeated eruptions. The chain’s defining pattern reflects the Pacific Plate moving northwest over a relatively fixed hotspot: Hawaiʻi Island lies above the active hotspot, while older volcanoes extend northwest. After volcanism weakens, erosion gradually reshapes the islands. This ongoing process makes the archipelago a clear example of how plate motion and volcanism can build a long island chain; politically, Hawaiʻi became the 50th U.S. state on August 21, 1959.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm\",\"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-hotspot-and-how-do-you-know-its-there\",\"https://www.archives.gov/legislative/features/hawaii\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"The Hawaiian Archipelago stretches about 1,500 miles across the central Pacific.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"The islands are exposed peaks of volcanic mountains built from the seafloor by repeated eruptions.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"The Pacific Plate’s movement over a relatively fixed hotspot helps explain the chain’s age pattern: Hawaiʻi Island is above the active hotspot, and older volcanoes lie northwest.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-hotspot-and-how-do-you-know-its-there\"]},{\"text\":\"As volcanic activity weakens, erosion reshapes the islands.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"Hawaiʻi became the 50th U.S. state on August 21, 1959.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.archives.gov/legislative/features/hawaii\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Geology & Volcanoes\",\"url\":\"https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm\",\"publisher\":\"National Park Service\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:37:18.243927+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Central Pacific location and 1,500-mile span; volcanic mountain peaks built by eruptions; erosion reshapes islands as volcanism wanes.\"},{\"title\":\"What is a hotspot and how do you know it's there?\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-hotspot-and-how-do-you-know-its-there\",\"publisher\":\"U.S. Geological Survey\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:37:18.243927+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Hotspot explanation, Pacific Plate movement, active hotspot beneath Hawaiʻi Island, and older volcanoes extending northwest.\"},{\"title\":\"Hawaii Statehood, August 21, 1959\",\"url\":\"https://www.archives.gov/legislative/features/hawaii\",\"publisher\":\"National Archives and Records Administration\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:37:18.243927+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Hawaiʻi became the 50th state on August 21, 1959.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:nps.gov Hawaiian Islands archipelago volcanic islands geography formation\",\"site:usgs.gov Hawaiian Islands hotspot chain islands geology\",\"site:hawaii.edu Hawaiian Islands geography archipelago islands youngest oldest\",\"site:archives.gov Hawaii became 50th state August 21 1959\"],\"notes\":\"The overview is 99 words. Each overview claim and key fact is linked to directly opened National Park Service, U.S. Geological Survey, or National Archives pages. The USGS source describes hotspot origins as not fully understood; the overview presents the relatively fixed hotspot as the explanation for the observed chain pattern, not as a settled account of hotspot origins.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
