    "overview": {
      "text": "The Hawaiian Islands are a volcanic archipelago in the central Pacific and include the islands of the U.S. state of Hawaiʻi. Their land is the exposed summits of volcanic mountains built up from the ocean floor by repeated eruptions. The chain’s defining pattern reflects the Pacific Plate moving northwest over a relatively fixed hotspot: Hawaiʻi Island lies above the active hotspot, while older volcanoes extend northwest. After volcanism weakens, erosion gradually reshapes the islands. This ongoing process makes the archipelago a clear example of how plate motion and volcanism can build a long island chain; politically, Hawaiʻi became the 50th U.S. state on August 21, 1959.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/locations/hawaii/geology.htm",
        "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-hotspot-and-how-do-you-know-its-there",
        "https://www.archives.gov/legislative/features/hawaii"
